(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:actor/wave 2), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "adr-2799005411-cloud-itonami-isco-5411-firefighters-coverage" "adr-2799005413-cloud-itonami-isco-5413-prison-guards-coverage" "adr-2792005412-cloud-itonami-isco-5412-police-officers-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "initiate the lift operation" "initiate the load movement" "move the load" "finalize the lift operation" "finalize the load movement" "authorize the load movement" "authorize load movement" "execute the load movement" "execute the lift operation" "commit to the load movement" "direct the lift operation" "operate the lifting truck" "operate the forklift" "drive the forklift" "drive the lifting truck" "confirm the load movement" "override the operator's safety judgment" "override the operator safety judgment" "override the operator's on-site safety judgment")), (:adr/id 2799008344), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-service-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:log-service-record :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-maintenance-order}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:log-service-record :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-maintenance-order :flag-safety-concern}, :auto #{:log-service-record :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-maintenance-order}}}"), (:actor/domain :logistics/lifting-truck-operations), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-service-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-maintenance-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :no-site :no-actuation :unknown-op :unknown-equipment :equipment-wrong-site :unknown-operator :operator-wrong-site :scope-exclusion)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "8344"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799008344: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8344 Lifting Truck Operators warehouse-crew scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8344\n-- Lifting Truck Operators** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8344\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any registry-side work in\nthis ADR (`clojure -M -e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println\n(kotoba.occupation/get-occupation \\\"8344\\\"))\"` from a checkout with\nthe `kotoba-lang/technology` sibling present at the correct\n`../technology` relative path), and its `:name` read exactly \"Lifting\nTruck Operators\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this\nADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`.\n\nThis is a RETRY of an earlier attempt that was cut off mid-task by a\nsession rate limit after creating the `cloud-itonami/\ncloud-itonami-isco-8344` GitHub repository but before pushing any actor\nsource (the repo held only default `gh repo create` boilerplate:\n`.gitignore`, `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `GOVERNANCE.md`,\n`LICENSE`). By the time this retry's registry/ADR work began, a\nconcurrent sibling agent in the same retry batch (up to eight sibling\nagents were landing concurrently against this fleet's shared\n`kotoba-lang/occupation` registry and 90-docs/adr tree this session)\nhad already pushed a complete `LiftingTruckOpsActor` implementation to\nthe repository's `main` branch (commit `adb5ff2`, \"feat(liftingtruckops):\nimplement LiftingTruckOpsActor (ISCO-08 8344)\"). This ADR's author\nindependently re-cloned that pushed repository fresh, read every file\nin full, and independently re-ran the test suite from a clean checkout\nbefore trusting it -- the implementation was not re-derived from\nscratch. The registry promotion and this ADR are performed by this\nagent as the remaining, previously-incomplete steps of the original\ntask.\n\n## CRITICAL safety framing: this actor coordinates SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY -- it never operates the lifting truck\n\nLifting Truck Operators (forklift drivers, warehouse lift operators)\noperate load-bearing equipment with real physical-safety stakes: load\ndrop, tip-over, pedestrian collision. This actor is **NOT** a\nlift-operation actor. It is a warehouse-crew scheduling/logistics\ncoordination robot. The closed proposal-op allowlist has **no op,\nanywhere in it, that could finalize a lift-operation/load-movement\ndecision or override an operator's on-site safety judgment** -- that\nauthority stays entirely with the certified human operator on site, at\nall times, under this actor's design and under any certified\ndeployment of it. This mirrors the closed-allowlist-plus-content-based-\nscope-exclusion safety pattern already established in this fleet by\n`cloud-itonami-isco-5411` (Firefighters) and `cloud-itonami-isco-5413`\n(Prison Guards): decisions with direct physical-safety stakes are\nexcluded from the closed op allowlist structurally, not merely gated\nbehind escalation.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/\n`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set). The closed\nop-allowlist + registered-provenance + content-based scope-exclusion\nSAFETY PATTERN specifically follows\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5411`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5411)\n(Firefighters) and its sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-5413` (Prison\nGuards) -- both independently re-cloned and re-read in full by this\nagent, in addition to the domain-closest existing sibling\n`cloud-itonami-isco-8343` (Crane, Hoist and Related Plant Operators,\nalready `:implemented`), which established the precedent that a\nlifting-equipment coordination actor in this fleet never actuates the\nequipment itself and independently re-verifies load/capacity data\nagainst the equipment's own registered record rather than trusting a\nproposal's self-report.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8344` as an OSS scheduling/\nlogistics-coordination actor for lifting-truck (forklift) fleet\noperations, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Warehouse\nLogistics Advisor ⊣ LiftingTruckOpsGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned\nidentically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a different library\ncoordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated\n`20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-service-record` -- lift-usage/incident-report data logging\n  (equipment id, record type, timestamp -- non-actuating documentation\n  only)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- operator-roster/warehouse-shift\n  scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface an equipment-defect/load-hazard/\n  operator-fatigue concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit-\n  eligible\n- `:coordinate-maintenance-order` -- lifting-truck maintenance\n  procurement proposal, gated by the equipment's own registered cost\n  ceiling\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**\noperating a lifting truck or finalizing a load movement -- these\ncapabilities are **structurally absent from the op-allowlist\nentirely**, not merely gated behind escalation. The reference mock\nadvisor's `infer` function only ever emits one of the four allowlist\nops (a `case`/map-shaped construction with no branch producing any\nother op), and if any code path (a forged proposal, a future LLM\nadvisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced an op outside the\nallowlist, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check (`:unknown-op`)\nrejects it unconditionally and permanently. Verified two ways: (a)\ngovernor-level unit tests against concretely-named forbidden-op\nfixtures (`:operate-lifting-truck`, `:finalize-load-movement`), and\n(b) an END-TO-END test that drives those same forbidden-op attempts,\nplus scope-excluded rationale attempts and a direct `:effect`\nactuation-escape attempt, through a ROGUE advisor and the FULL\n`langgraph.graph` actor graph (not just the governor's pure check\nfunction), proving the StateGraph itself always resolves to `:hold`\nwith zero records committed even when the advisor node itself is\ncompromised (`liftingtruckops.actor-test/\nrogue-advisor-proposals-always-hold-with-zero-records-committed`).\n\n### Six HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Site unregistered** (`:no-site`) -- any proposal must resolve to\n   an independently registered warehouse/site record. Never trusts the\n   proposal's own claim of which site is involved.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** (`:no-actuation`) -- any other `:effect`\n   value is a HARD block (a claim to directly actuate outside\n   governance; the governor never dispatches hardware and never\n   operates the lifting truck).\n3. **Op not allowed** (`:unknown-op`) -- any `:op` outside the closed\n   four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This independently\n   rejects any attempt to slip in an `:operate-lifting-truck`/\n   `:finalize-load-movement`/`:move-load`-shaped op.\n4. **Equipment basis** (`:unknown-equipment` / `:equipment-wrong-\n   site`) -- `:log-service-record` and `:coordinate-maintenance-order`\n   must cite a REGISTERED lifting truck belonging to the requesting\n   site; a missing, unknown, or wrong-site `:equipment-id` is a HARD\n   block.\n5. **Operator basis** (`:unknown-operator` / `:operator-wrong-site`)\n   -- `:schedule-crew-operation` must cite a REGISTERED operator\n   belonging to the requesting site; a missing, unknown, or wrong-site\n   `:operator-id` is a HARD block.\n6. **Scope exclusion** (`:scope-exclusion`) -- independent of the\n   op-allowlist check, any proposal whose free text (`:rationale` or\n   `:detail`) names a finalization/execution action for a lift\n   operation, a load movement, or an override of an operator's\n   on-site safety judgment (nineteen phrases in\n   `liftingtruckops.governor`'s private `scope-excluded-phrases`, e.g.\n   \"initiate the lift operation\", \"move the load\", \"operate the\n   lifting truck\", \"override the operator's safety judgment\") is a\n   HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated unconditionally on content,\n   case-insensitively. This is defense-in-depth on top of check 3:\n   even if a future change somehow widened the allowlist, content-\n   based rejection still independently blocks lift-operation/\n   load-movement finalization language wherever it appears in free\n   text.\n\n### Escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface an equipment-defect/load-\n  hazard/operator-fatigue observation -- it never resolves the\n  observation itself.\n- a `:coordinate-maintenance-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds the\n  cited equipment's OWN registered `:max-maintenance-cost` -- always\n  needs human sign-off, independent of confidence. Unlike the fixed\n  governor-level numeric threshold used by `cloud-itonami-isco-5411`/\n  `-5413` (a single constant such as 500 for every site), this\n  governor independently re-reads the ceiling from the equipment's\n  own registered record (ground truth), never a proposal self-report\n  -- the same two-sided range-check discipline `cloud-itonami-isco-\n  8343`'s `craneoperations.governor` uses for rated-capacity, applied\n  here to a maintenance-cost ceiling instead of a load value. Verified\n  boundary-inclusive (`ok-at-exact-ceiling-boundary`).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"lift\", \"load\") can accidentally match inside the mock advisor's\nown default rationale text for a legitimate proposal, self-blocking\nthe actor's own happy path -- a real risk here since every proposal in\nthis vertical necessarily discusses lift-truck/load/equipment\nvocabulary to do its coordination job.\n`liftingtruckops.governor`'s `scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (\"initiate the lift\noperation\", \"move the load\", \"operate the lifting truck\",\n\"override the operator's safety judgment\" -- the exact phrasing\npattern the task guidance for this bug class calls for), never bare\nnouns, and `liftingtruckops.governor-test/\ndefault-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion` is a\ndedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed allowlist,\ndriven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-exclusion`. The mock advisor's own default rationale template\n(\"proposed <op> for site <id>\") was written to avoid any bare-noun\ncollision by construction -- it never names a finalization/execution\naction, only the coordination op being proposed. Confirmed green on\nfirst run against a fresh clone by this agent; the self-trip guard was\nnever observed tripping during independent re-verification.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8344\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8344` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8344`), via the mandated Contents-API-PUT-with-\n  blob-SHA-CAS discipline (registry.edn is a single-line datomized\n  blob; branch+merge is unreliable for it). `maturity-summary`'s\n  implemented count bumped accordingly, recomputed live from the\n  registry via `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary` immediately before\n  the edit (not assumed) to account for concurrent sibling landings in\n  this same retry batch.\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`) independently re-verified by\n  this agent against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository\n  (not the sibling agent's working copy): 27 tests, 76 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report.\n- No implementation bugs were found during this agent's independent\n  audit of the already-pushed code; no assertions were weakened.\n- `clj-kondo` lint was not run separately -- this actor's `deps.edn`\n  does not define a `:lint` alias, matching the verified\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3313`/`-5411`/`-5413` references' own drift (the\n  `:test` alias with `cognitect-labs/test-runner` is the only alias\n  defined).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- consistent with the live convention already confirmed\n  by sibling ISCO actors this batch; only the central `kotoba-lang/\n  occupation` registry itself depends on it, to resolve\n  `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- The already-pushed repository's `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`/`SECURITY.md`/\n  `GOVERNANCE.md`/`CONTRIBUTING.md` say \"cloud-itonami organization\"\n  for private-contact routing (matching the factually-correct org name\n  the repository is actually published under, the same fix already\n  present in the `-5411`/`-5413` siblings, not the stale \"gftdcojp\n  organization\" text still present in the older `cloud-itonami-isco-\n  3313` reference).\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-lift-operation`/`:approve-\n  load-movement` that is merely gated behind `always-escalate-ops`\n  (the shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for `:approve-over-\n  ceiling-posting`): rejected outright per the explicit task guidance\n  and the `cloud-itonami-isco-5411`/`-5413` precedent -- an\n  always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize a lift\n  operation or load movement exists in the code path and is merely\n  deferred to a human gate. The task is explicit that this must be a\n  hard permanent block or always-escalate op, never auto-commit-\n  eligible, and the implementation goes further: the capability itself\n  does not exist in the allowlist at all. The only op related to\n  safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need\n  for human attention and never itself decides, authorizes, or defers\n  a lift-operation action for later automatic execution.\n- A fixed governor-level numeric maintenance-cost threshold (the\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-5411`/`-5413` shape, a single constant such as\n  500 applied uniformly to every site): considered, but the\n  implementation instead reads the ceiling from each piece of\n  equipment's own registered `:max-maintenance-cost` -- more\n  consistent with `cloud-itonami-isco-3313`'s per-account\n  `:max-transaction-amount` ceiling shape and with `cloud-itonami-\n  isco-8343`'s own-record ground-truth range-check discipline. This\n  ADR records this as an intentional design choice rather than a\n  deviation to fix, since it more accurately models real fleets where\n  different lifting trucks warrant different maintenance-spend\n  ceilings.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"lift\" \"load\"\n  \"forklift\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above, and per the task's explicit worked examples\n  of the correct action-phrase form -- this actor's own domain\n  necessarily discusses lift-truck/load/equipment concepts to do its\n  coordination job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the\n  actor's own legitimate proposals.\n- Re-implementing the actor from scratch rather than auditing and\n  building on the already-pushed sibling-agent implementation:\n  rejected -- the pushed code was independently re-cloned, read in\n  full, and re-tested from a clean checkout by this agent before being\n  trusted, and it correctly implements every requirement in this\n  ADR's Decision section; duplicating it would have produced a\n  divergent, unreviewed second implementation for no safety or quality\n  benefit.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-service-record`** -- lift-usage/incident-report data\n   logging against a registered piece of equipment. Not a lift-\n   operation or load-movement action in any respect -- it is a\n   records-management action, HARD-blocked from proceeding without a\n   registered, same-site equipment record (`:unknown-equipment` /\n   `:equipment-wrong-site`).\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- operator-roster/warehouse-shift\n   scheduling logistics only, against a registered, same-site operator\n   record (`:unknown-operator` / `:operator-wrong-site`). Does not\n   plan, authorize, or draw any conclusion about an actual lift or\n   load movement -- it is a calendar action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces an equipment-defect/load-\n   hazard/operator-fatigue concern to a human; ALWAYS escalates\n   (governor's `always-escalate-ops`, unconditionally, at every\n   confidence level), NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY\n   channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it\n   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself --\n   the human reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-maintenance-order`** -- lifting-truck maintenance\n   procurement proposal against a registered, same-site equipment\n   record, gated by that equipment's own registered cost ceiling\n   (`:max-maintenance-cost`, ground truth, never the proposal's\n   self-report). Not a lift-operation or load-movement action of any\n   kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics action for maintenance\n   service, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation like any\n   equipment procurement workflow.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase,\nresembles operating a lifting truck or finalizing a lift-operation/\nload-movement decision, or overriding an operator's on-site safety\njudgment -- these are structurally absent, not gated, confirmed by:\n(a) the closed four-op allowlist itself (`liftingtruckops.governor`'s\nprivate `allowed-ops`), (b) concretely-named forbidden-op fixtures\nrejected at both the governor-unit level and the full-actor-graph\nlevel via a rogue advisor\n(`liftingtruckops.governor-test/hard-on-unknown-op`,\n`liftingtruckops.actor-test/rogue-advisor-proposals-always-hold-with-zero-records-committed`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`liftingtruckops.governor`'s private `scope-excluded?`/\n`scope-excluded-phrases`), tested both directly against the governor\nand end-to-end through a rogue advisor and the full compiled graph."), (:adr/date "2026-07-17"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-maintenance-order :equipment/registered-max-maintenance-cost}"), (:test/counts "{:total 27, :assertions 76, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Lifting Truck Operators Warehouse Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :site-provenance :equipment-provenance :operator-provenance :closed-op-allowlist :maintenance-cost-threshold-escalation :rogue-advisor-end-to-end)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-8344"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8344"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8344)), (:test/simulator :liftingtruckops.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-8344 — Lifting Truck Operators Warehouse Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-8344)))